WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.402
Runsite begins with a simple details: a location is not ready just because a map exists.

2
00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:12.804
A flat floor plan can tell the table where the walls are, but it cannot tell the crew where the pressure lives.

3
00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:19.205
Runsite turns a mission space into something the players can read before the first door opens: approach routes, exposure, exits, camera lines, chokepoints, and the places where a quiet plan starts becoming loud.

4
00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:25.607
The player view stays clean enough to act on. The GM view keeps the teeth behind the curtain.

5
00:00:25.607 --> 00:00:32.009
A warehouse stops being a rectangle and becomes a layered problem: outside light, security habits, blind corners, maintenance paths, alarms that matter, and consequences waiting just off-screen.

6
00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:38.411
The point is not to script the route. The point is to make the site strong enough for improvisation.

7
00:00:38.411 --> 00:00:44.812
If the crew scouts, the space rewards attention. If they rush, the space pushes back.

8
00:00:44.812 --> 00:00:51.214
If they split up, the table still understands what each choice costs.

9
00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:57.616
Runsite gives the GM a place that can breathe under pressure and gives the players a location worth planning around.

10
00:00:57.616 --> 00:01:04.018
A good runsite also carries time: patrol rhythm, response delay, exit pressure, environmental risk, and what changes when the crew trips the wrong line.

11
00:01:04.018 --> 00:01:10.420
It should help the table ask better questions before the dice start: where can we enter, what can see us, what can trap us, and what do we lose if we wait.

12
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:16.821
The goal is not a prettier map.

13
00:01:16.821 --> 00:01:23.223
It is a shared tactical memory that keeps the fiction moving when the plan meets resistance.

14
00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:29.625
Before the breach, during the run, and after the fallout, the site remains legible, dangerous, and memorable.
